# Progressive Web App

`cargo rullst make:pwa` made this application installable. The files are
yours to change; running the command again never overwrites them.

| File | Purpose |
| :--- | :--- |
| `static/manifest.webmanifest` | Name, colours, start URL and icons that browsers and stores read. |
| `static/icons/` | Placeholder icons (the Rullst logo). Replace them with your own PNG files of the same sizes. |
| `src/pwa.rs` | Serves `/sw.js` and `/offline`; `head()` holds the tags each layout includes. |
| `src/pwa/sw.js` | The service worker. |
| `static/pwa.js` | Registers the worker and shows the update prompt. |

## Before release

1. Replace the four icons in `static/icons/`. `maskable-512.png` must keep
   its subject inside the central circle of 80% of the width, because
   launchers crop it to their own shape.
2. Serve the application over HTTPS. Browsers register service workers only
   on HTTPS origins and on `localhost`.
3. Bump `version` in `Cargo.toml` with each release. The worker names its
   cache after it, so users get a "new version" prompt and a fresh offline
   copy; without a version change they keep the files stored at install.

## What the worker stores

- Navigations go to the network first. A page is stored for offline use only
  when its response allows it; Rullst answers `Cache-Control: no-store` by
  default, so personal and authenticated pages are never kept on the device.
  Without the network the worker shows a stored copy or `/offline`.
- Files under `/static/` whose names carry a content hash
  (`app.3f9a2c1b.css`) are served from the cache first; other static files
  go to the network first and fall back to the stored copy.
- Requests other than GET, other origins, forms, htmx fragments and APIs are
  never stored.

## Data while offline

Keep the server authoritative. If a page must accept changes offline, queue
them in IndexedDB with an idempotency key and send them again when the
connection returns; the server accepts each key once and rejects stale
writes. The PWA guide shows the pattern:
https://rullst.github.io/Rullst/book/tutorials/43-progressive-web-apps.html
## Stores

- Google Play: `cargo rullst make:pwa --android --host <host> --package-id
  <name>` adds a Bubblewrap configuration for a Trusted Web Activity and
  `PWA-ANDROID.md` with its steps.
- Microsoft Store: package the same site with PWABuilder.
- Apple App Store: not offered; iOS users add the app to the home screen
  from Safari.

The PWA guide covers each step.
